  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 254 AM EDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nWednesday - Thursday Night...\n\nElongated upper ridging will be in control across the southern half \nof the CONUS on Wednesday, with a belt of zonal westerlies \ncontrolling the northern half of the CONUS. At the surface, a \nnotably strong pressure gradient will exist across the Ohio River \nValley between surface high pressure anchored off the mid-Atlantic \ncoast and a strong surface low over eastern Canada. This will give \nus a steady to occasionally gusty SW flow with peak gusts likely in \nthe 20-25 mph range through the afternoon. This will be the hottest \nday of the period with temps warming into the low and mid 90s across \nthe area. Dew points are also expected to \"pool\" across our NW CWA a \nbit ahead of the approaching cold front, but this will also be the \narea closes to upstream convection and afternoon cloud cover could \nplay a limiting role. Regardless, max heat index values will be into \nthe triple digits along and west of I-65 at some point Wednesday \nafternoon. For now, looks like we'll stay just short of any headline \ncriteria for most of the area, but will continue to monitor T/Td \ntrends as it will be close in our NW CWA.\n\nAs we move into later Wednesday evening through Thursday, we'll deal \nwith fairly widespread convective activity along a cold front \ntrailing from the aforementioned eastern Canada surface low. Fairly \nimpressive late summer speed shear profile (25-35 knots) will be in \nplace across our northern CWA thanks to strong mid to upper level \njetting, and a decent low level jet response. If we do get a \nstronger updraft, then this would be sufficient for some \norganization, and perhaps an isolated strong to severe threat. Main \nproblem is that instability looks pretty meager later Wednesday \nevening into Wednesday night. By the time modest instability \ndevelops on Thursday, the overall shear profile is a bit weaker \nacross central and southern KY. Can't rule out a few strong to \nsevere storms later Wednesday evening into Thursday, but overall not \nreal impressed with the overall scenario, and SPC Marginal Risk \nseems appropriate.\n\nWill also have to watch for the potential for a few swaths of \nheavier rain through Thursday night, even if it might be welcome in \nmost locations. The overall orientation of the deep layer shear \nprofile will be roughly parallel to the surface frontal boundary, \nand this could allow for some training/repeated rounds of showers or \nstorms. 07/12z LREF data did increase probabilities of over 1\" of \nrain from 10-15% to 20-40% along and north of the I-64 corridor. \nThis is a notable increase, and have also noted that a few of the \ndeterministic models show some isolated swaths of 2-3\". These values \nstill aren't overly concerning given how dry we've been, but \nnevertheless something to monitor for trends.\n\nFriday - Monday...\n\nThe late week into the weekend time frame now looks a lot more \nactive than it did 24 hours ago as the overall frontal progression \nhas slowed, and now lingers across KY into the weekend. In addition, \na slow moving shortwave on the southern end of the stronger belt of \nwesterlies looks to meander out of the southern Plains into the \nTennessee Valley, and could be a spark for additional diurnally driven \nshowers and storms through the weekend mainly across central and \nsouthern KY. Will keep lingering iso-sct chance pops in the forecast \nthrough the weekend to account for this.\n\nAfter a brief cool down with temps in the low and mid 80s behind the \nfront on Friday, max T's should quickly recover to the upper 80s to \naround 90 through the weekend.",
